    "result_text": "Rates can now carry a from-date, and all 4 tests in `bin/e2e` pass.\n\n**How it behaves**\n- A claim's due uses its grade's rate with the latest from-date on or before the claim's date. A rate with no from-date applies from the beginning of time. If no rate applies yet, the due shows `n/a`.\n- Settling a claim fixes its due using the rate in force on the claim's date, not the rate in force when it is settled. When no rate applies, the audit line ends `due=n/a`.\n- Setting a rate for a grade and from-date that already exist replaces it.\n- `RATE_SET` ends with `from=<date>` only when a from-date was given.\n\n**One thing that may surprise you:** a person's due total is not the sum of their claims' dues. It is their total claimed times the rate in force on the sheet's today. The tests require this: Ada's claims show 1.00 + 3.00 + 3.00 = 7.00, but her expected total is 9.00 (30.00 claimed \u00d7 30%). The sheet's due total adds up these per-person figures. If you want the person total to add up the per-claim dues instead, the tests would need to change.\n\n**Changes**\n- **Database:** a new migration, `V10__rate_from.sql`. It adds a nullable `from_date` column and gives each rate its own id. A unique constraint on (sheet, grade, from-date) treats two blank from-dates as the same, so a grade can't have two start rates.\n- **Server:** `Rate.java` has the logic that picks the rate in force on a date, used when settling (`ClaimService`). The rates endpoint takes and returns `from`.\n- **Front end:**\n  - The rate form has an optional From date field.\n  - The rate list has one row per grade and from-date (`rate-row-<grade>-<from>`). Each grade keeps its `rate-row-<grade>-start` row, which shows `n/a` when that grade has no start rate.\n  - A new \"In force on <today>\" table carries `rate-current-<grade>`.\n  - Claim dues are worked out using each claim's own date.",
